ABSTRACT = {
 "Objectives": "To identify Korean districts where national health screening flags more low haemoglobin than expected, and accompanying district characteristics. Inference concerns districts, not individuals.",
 "Methods": f"Ecological panel of {N['exam_total']/1e6:.0f} million screening records of adults in {N['n_districts']} districts, 2011–2024. Flags (haemoglobin below 12.0 g/dL in men and 10.0 g/dL in women) were compared with published haemoglobin distributions; Bayesian spatiotemporal models estimated district associations.",
 "Results": f"Flag rates tracked below-threshold proportions, with nationwide steps in their ratio. Standardised ratios showed a persistent southern excess. Districts with more attendees with diagnosed diabetes had higher ratios in the principal model (rate ratio per SD {M[DM]['IRR']:.2f}) and in all three period-restricted analyses. Districts with more older adults living alone had higher ratios overall ({M[OLD]['IRR']:.2f}) but not credibly in 2018–2024 ({JW(OLD, 'J3_2018_24')[0]:.2f}). Physical activity showed no consistent association.",
 "Discussion": "These patterns locate districts for assessing the follow-up of abnormal screening results; they do not establish individual causes.",
}

INTRO = [
 f"Anaemia affected almost a quarter of the world's population in 2021, dietary iron deficiency was its leading cause, and its burden varies widely by age, sex and place [@gbd2023prevalence]. In the United States, prevalence exceeded 20% at age 85 years or older, and nutrient deficiency, chronic inflammation or renal disease, and unexplained anaemia each accounted for about one-third of anaemia in older people [@guralnik2004prevalence]; renal insufficiency, inflammation and an age-related decline in erythropoietin production contribute [@stauder2018anemia]. In older people, anaemia is associated with a decline in physical performance [@penninx2003anemia].",
 f"In a Korean national survey, older adults with anaemia more often lived alone, received social assistance or had diabetes or chronic renal failure [@chueh2020high]; in Korean adults diabetes was associated with anaemia [@kim2021association], and in older adults nutritional anaemia was associated with lower nutrient intake [@park2019comparison]. Iron deficiency remains common worldwide [@pasricha2021iron], and thalassaemia carriage has been found in young people of multiethnic background in Korea [@lee2019increased]. These individual-level studies do not identify the districts in which screening flags more low haemoglobin than expected for the sex and age of attendees, which is what is needed to decide where follow-up of abnormal results should be strengthened.",
 f"Korea's National Health Insurance Service screens tens of millions of adults [@seong2017cohort], and the national statistics portal reports each year the number of screening records flagged as suspected anaemia in every district [@web:kosis2026screening]. District-level clustering of anaemia has been shown among rural men in India [@singh2024district], but, to the author's knowledge, routine haemoglobin screening of a national adult population has not been used to map where low haemoglobin accumulates and which area characteristics accompany it. Area characteristics plausibly matter: living alone is associated with malnutrition risk in older people [@besoramoreno2020social], food insecurity with micronutrient deficiency and anaemia [@lopes2023food], and nutrition-label reading with healthier micronutrient intake [@kim2014association], whereas smoking raises haemoglobin and can mask anaemia [@nordenberg1990effect].",
 f"This study treats the district, not the person, as the unit of inference. District associations describe places and cannot be transferred to the people who live in them [@morgenstern1995ecologic; @greenland1989ecological]; the study therefore asks where in Korea screening flags more low haemoglobin than expected for the sex and age of attendees, how stable that geography is, and which district characteristics of deprivation, household composition, chronic disease, lifestyle and access accompany it. Because a screening flag is an administrative judgement rather than a measurement, the flag was first reconciled with the published distribution of measured haemoglobin, to separate changes in judgement practice from changes in haemoglobin.",
]

METHODS = [
("Design, outcome and population",
 f"This ecological panel study used annual district statistics of the general health screening programme of the National Health Insurance Service [@seong2017cohort]. For each district and year, the number of screening records with a recorded judgement and the number flagged as suspected anaemia were taken from the national statistics portal, as were national numbers by sex and age group [@web:kosis2026screening]. Under the Ministry of Health and Welfare's standards for health screening, a haemoglobin concentration below 12.0 g/dL in men and below 10.0 g/dL in women is judged as suspected anaemia, and 12.0–12.9 g/dL and 10.0–11.9 g/dL as borderline; no other test enters this judgement and there is no upper threshold [@web:mohw2024standards]. The threshold for women is lower than the 12.0 g/dL commonly used to define anaemia in non-pregnant women [@pasricha2018revisiting], so in women the flag excludes mild anaemia. The judgement uses only a lower cut-off; high haemoglobin is not flagged as suspected anaemia. Counts are screening records, not unique individuals; people may attend in several years. Districts were harmonised to 2024 boundaries. Sejong, reported separately in only 1 of 14 years, was excluded from estimation but kept as an unobserved district in the adjacency graph, leaving {N['n_districts']} districts and {N['n_rows']:,} district-years for 2011–2024 ({sup('methods')})."),
("Judgement check",
 f"Because the flag is an administrative judgement, the annual national and provincial numbers flagged in each sex were compared with the numbers of attendees whose measured haemoglobin fell below the same thresholds in the published haemoglobin distribution tables [@web:kosis2026hb]. A stable ratio indicates a stable aggregate relationship between flagged and below-threshold records, not exact agreement; a step indicates a discontinuity in that relationship, whose administrative or reporting cause cannot be established from these data. Expected numbers computed from the national positivity of each year account for such steps only to the extent that they applied similarly across districts ({sup('judgement')})."),
("Sex and age standardisation",
 f"Expected numbers flagged were obtained by indirect standardisation [@inskip1983methods]. National positivity in each year, sex and age group (19 years or younger, 5-year groups from 20–24 to 80–84 years, and 85 years or older) was applied to the estimated age structure of each district's attendees, derived from the resident population by sex and 5-year age group [@web:mois2026population] and national uptake by sex and age, and rescaled to the observed number of attendees of each sex. The standardised anaemia ratio is the observed over the expected number. Using the national positivity of the same year accounts for nationwide changes in positivity or in judgement practice, but not for changes in selection or measurement that differ between districts."),
("District characteristics",
 f"District characteristics and their expected directions were fixed before any covariate model was fitted, on the basis of the epidemiology of anaemia. Deprivation, household composition and nutrition-label literacy: basic livelihood security receipt (+) [@chueh2020high; @lopes2023food], tertiary education (−), nutrition-label literacy (−) [@kim2014association] and the share of households of older adults living alone (+) [@chueh2020high; @besoramoreno2020social]. Women and childbearing: the share of marriages involving a foreign spouse (+) [@lee2019increased] and the total fertility rate (+) [@pasricha2021iron]. Chronic disease: the share of attendees with diagnosed diabetes (+) [@thomas2003unrecognized; @kim2021association; @stauffer2014prevalence]. Lifestyle and physical activity from the Community Health Survey: current smoking (−) [@nordenberg1990effect], high-risk drinking (+), and walking and moderate-or-vigorous physical activity (−, hypothesised through better general health; population evidence is scarce). Access and selection: internal medicine specialists per 100,000 population (−) and screening uptake (−, with healthier people attending where uptake is high). Five controls were forced into every model: urban population share, physicians per 1,000 population, sex ratio, fiscal autonomy and the share aged 80 years or older. Diagnosed diabetes among attendees and the share of older adults living alone are crude shares, not age-standardised, and partly mark district age structure. Covariates were standardised to 1 standard deviation (SD). The share of older adults living alone was observed from 2015; its 2011–2014 values (912 district-years) were filled with district means, so within-district variation in this characteristic arises only from 2015. Tertiary education was observed only in the 2015 and 2020 censuses and was interpolated or held at the nearest census value; other gaps were filled within districts ({sup('covariates')}; checks in {sup('checks')})."),
("Statistical analysis",
 f"The likelihood (Poisson or negative binomial) was chosen by the lower widely applicable information criterion (WAIC) of the forced-controls model. Models had an offset of log expected numbers, a Besag–York–Mollié 2 (BYM2) district effect with penalised-complexity priors [@riebler2016intuitive] and random-walk and independent year effects, and were fitted by integrated nested Laplace approximation [@rue2009approximate] in R 4.6.0. Rate ratios (RRs) per 1 SD are reported with 95% credible intervals (CrIs); an association was credible when the interval excluded 1. RRs are mutually adjusted ecological associations, not individual or causal effects [@morgenstern1995ecologic]. No multiplicity adjustment was applied, and associations were interpreted as exploratory, in light of their size and robustness. Following the multiverse approach [@steegen2016increasing], the model was refitted with 200 random covariate subsets; a covariate was subset-stable if credible in at least 75% of the subsets containing it, with the same sign in at least 95%. Residual hot and cold spots had a posterior probability greater than 0.95 or less than 0.05 that the residual spatial RR exceeded 1, for the whole period and for two windows separated by the 2016–2017 change in national positivity (2011–2016 and 2017–2024)."),
("Sensitivity analyses",
 f"Knorr-Held type I–IV space–time interactions were added [@knorrheld2000bayesian]; subset-stable covariates were split into district means and within-district deviations [@bell2015explaining]; the model was refitted in each window and with covariate-by-window interactions in a single model sharing one spatial effect and set of controls across windows; the model was refitted after excluding 2011–2012, when the ratio of flagged to below-threshold records was lowest in men (2013–2024), and separately to 2013–2017 and 2018–2024, either side of the largest step in that ratio; and expected numbers were recomputed from the resident population instead of attendees, which changes the estimand from positivity among attendees to flagged records per resident. Further specifications varied the likelihood, priors, neighbourhoods, controls, lags, left-out provinces or years and filled values ({sup('specs')}). Treated gastric cancer and infectious disease (district counts of people treated, from the same statistics portal) served as comparison outcomes and hit-and-run accidents, fires and household waste recycling as negative-control exposures [@lipsitch2010negative]; E-values indicated the strength of unmeasured district-level confounding needed to explain each association [@vanderweele2017sensitivity]. After the results were seen, associations against the expected direction were examined post hoc with the between–within decomposition, rank correlations of district means and correlations among covariates ({sup('posthoc')})."),
("Ethics and reporting",
 "The study used only published aggregate statistics and involved no individual data. The institutional review board of the author's university approved the study and waived informed consent (details on the title page), and the study was conducted in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki. The study follows the STROBE statement [@vonelm2007strengthening] (" + sup("strobe") + ")."),
]

