DISCUSSION = [
 "After standardisation for sex and age, low haemoglobin flagged at screening showed a largely stable divide, with excess in the south-west and south-east, many hot spots being rural counties, and deficits in the capital region. Provincial flag rates tracked the proportion of attendees with haemoglobin below the published thresholds, although the ratio of flagged to below-threshold records shifted in nationwide steps, and the fall in positivity between 2016 and 2017 accompanied a smaller proportion of attendees below the thresholds rather than a change in that ratio. Districts with more attendees with diagnosed diabetes had more flagged low haemoglobin in most analyses; the larger association with the share of older adults living alone was not credible in 2018–2024; physical activity showed no consistent association.",
 "### Interpreting district-level associations",
 "These are statements about districts. They show where flagged low haemoglobin is concentrated and which district characteristics travel with it; they do not show that older people who live alone, or people with diabetes, are themselves more often anaemic, because district associations can arise from people other than those with the characteristic and from district-level confounding [@morgenstern1995ecologic; @greenland1989ecological]. The flag also covers attendees of all adult ages, so district excess need not arise among older adults. The maps can locate districts in which the follow-up of abnormal haemoglobin results deserves assessment and generate hypotheses for individual-level studies; they do not establish unmet need, the effectiveness of additional follow-up or excess risk among older adults.",
 "### Diabetes",
 "Districts with a larger share of attendees with diagnosed diabetes had higher ratios between and within districts and in all three period-restricted refits, although not in the 2011–2016 window, which includes the transitional years of judgement for men. Anaemia is common in diabetes, especially with albuminuria or reduced kidney function [@thomas2003unrecognized], is associated with diabetes in Korean adults [@kim2021association] and rises with the stage of chronic kidney disease [@stauffer2014prevalence]; renal insufficiency, inflammation and reduced erythropoietin production contribute in older people [@stauder2018anemia]. The association was credible only with the share aged 80 years or older in the model, a highly collinear control, so it depends on that adjustment; it was also present, more weakly, for treated gastric cancer, so it may partly mark district differences in the health of attendees. Whether people flagged with anaemia were those with diabetes cannot be known from district data.",
 "### Older adults living alone",
 "The association with the share of older adults living alone was the largest in the principal model and persisted in all interaction models, but in 2018–2024 the estimate was small and not credible, so that period did not support a positive association; it was also smaller in window-specific models and when filled values were perturbed. This share was observed only from 2015, with district means filled for earlier years, and changes little within districts, which may add uncertainty in short periods, but the analysis does not establish why the estimate was attenuated. Individual studies suggest a pathway: living alone is associated with malnutrition risk in older people [@besoramoreno2020social], older Korean adults with anaemia more often live alone [@chueh2020high], nutritional anaemia is associated with lower nutrient intake [@park2019comparison] and nutrient deficiency is a recognised cause of anaemia in older people [@guralnik2004prevalence]. The screening statistics contain no information on diet, iron status or the cause of anaemia, and district associations cannot show that the people living alone were those flagged, so this pathway remains a hypothesis. Because this share was highly collinear with the share of the oldest residents, part of it may reflect very old age not removed by standardisation with an open-ended top age group. The small inverse association with nutrition-label literacy was not credible in the best-fitting interaction model and was no larger than those of negative-control exposures, so it cannot be distinguished from residual confounding [@kim2014association].",
 "### Other characteristics",
 "District averages of self-reported physical activity may be too coarse to show an individual effect; the small positive association of moderate-or-vigorous activity in 2018–2024 ran against the expected direction and was not seen in the principal model. Smoking, expected to mask anaemia by raising haemoglobin [@nordenberg1990effect], and high-risk drinking were not associated; foreign-spouse marriages were not associated either, although thalassaemia carriage has been found among young people of multiethnic background [@lee2019increased]. Tertiary education was inversely associated in two interaction models and in the later window but not in the principal model, which leaves its role uncertain. Higher uptake was associated with more anaemia, against the expected direction; the association arose within districts over time, was shared by treated gastric cancer, was absent from two interaction models and from 2018–2024, whereas district means ranked in the expected direction. This is compatible with changes in who attends or in ascertainment as uptake rises, since adults of lower socioeconomic status attend check-ups less often [@shin2018association]. The unstable inverse associations with basic livelihood security and fertility reversed the direction of their simple correlations, possibly through collinearity with living alone or residual urban–rural differences.",
 "### Strengths and limitations",
 "The study covered every harmonised district except Sejong with consistent statistics over the whole period, standardised expected numbers for sex and age within the screened population, and reconciled the screening flag with the published distribution of measured haemoglobin before modelling; the findings were tested in many sensitivity analyses. The ratio of flagged to below-threshold records shifted in nationwide steps, in men in 2013 and in both sexes in 2015 and 2018; these steps varied little between provinces, and year-specific expected numbers account for them only to the extent that they applied similarly across districts. The aggregate reconciliation does not show how the criterion was applied in each year, and flagged records exceeded below-threshold records in women from 2018 for reasons that could not be established. The outcome is a programme flag from a single haemoglobin measurement, without cause, severity or treatment, and its threshold for women is lower than survey definitions of anaemia. The flag covers all adult ages, and age-specific district data were unavailable, so district excess may arise in younger as well as older attendees. Records are not unique people, and the attendee age structure was estimated from residents and national uptake. Covariates differ in quality, living alone was filled for the early years, collinearity among ageing-related characteristics was high and predictive distributions were imperfectly calibrated. No data on diet or iron status were available, and adding the district characteristics worsened predictive fit over the spatial and temporal effects, so they describe rather than explain the geography. The credible negative-control exposures and small E-values show that modest residual district-level confounding could explain any of the associations.",
 "### Conclusion",
 "Low haemoglobin flagged at Korean health screening follows a persistent southern geography after sex and age are taken into account, and provincial flag rates track measured haemoglobin. At district level it is more common where many attendees have diabetes, an association that depended on adjustment for the share of the oldest residents; its association with the share of older adults living alone was not credible in 2018–2024. These districts are candidates for assessing the follow-up of abnormal haemoglobin results, and the findings generate hypotheses for individual-level studies of diabetes, diet and iron status.",
]

